 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**The cash rate has risen by about 4.25 percentage points since January 2022, and Sydney rents are growing at 3.5% a year (August 2026).**\n\n**Cash rate (RBA target, monthly average)**\n- **January 2022:** 0.10%\n- **September 2026:** 4.36%, which is **4.26 points higher** than in January 2022.\n\nIt didn't rise in a straight line:\n- **Hikes, May 2022 to December 2023:** up from 0.10% to 4.35%.\n- **Steady:** 4.35% through all of 2024.\n- **Cuts, February to September 2025:** down to 3.60%.\n- **Hikes again, February to June 2026:** back up to 4.35%.\n- **September 2026:** the average was 4.36%. That small rise suggests a change late in the month, but this monthly series can't confirm it.\n\n**Sydney rents (ABS monthly CPI, change from a year earlier)**\n- **Latest, August 2026:** **3.5%**\n- **A year earlier:** rent growth slowed from 5.6% in January 2025 to 3.2% in August–September 2025.\n- **Since then:** it has stayed between 3.3% and 3.5%, and has been 3.5% for June, July and August 2026.\n\nSo Sydney rents are still rising, but more slowly than in early 2025. Rent growth has stayed about the same even though rates went up again in 2026.",
